  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A warm front tomorrow will bring the next \nchance for widespread rain. \n\nHigh pressure moves offshore with southerly return flow behind \na warm front will bring dew points into the low 70s. Showers and \nthunderstorms will start moving into western areas before \ndaybreak and continue through the day. CAMS producing some \norganization to convective activity largely focused on the I-95 \nurban corridor. \n\nAs for the hazards, there is a SLIGHT (2 out of 4) risk for \nexcessive rainfall. Precipitable water values increase to \nperhaps as high as 150% of normal for this time of year. \nThe hires guidance suggests that heaviest precipitation will be\ngenerally along the I-95 corridor and points northwest. Hires\nguidance has also pointed to areas along the I-78 corridor\npotentially seeing heavy rainfall amounts as well. Generally \naround 1-2 inches of rain with localized QPF values in excess of\n3 inches. Widespread flash flooding still seems unlikely, but \nthe localized threat remains elevated. The Flood Watch was \nexpanded to include the Lehigh Valley and Berks county and\nremains in effect for northern NJ and the I-95 corridor.\n\nFor severe hazards, latest CAMS and HREF guidance show SBCAPE\nvalues remaining around 1500-2500 J/kg with ensemble maximums \nin excess 3000 J/kg. Deep layer shear forecasts remain around \n30kt with a strongly veering profile aloft. Boundary layer flow\nis not particularly strong but surface winds are more backed \nthan previous forecasts so an isolated tornado is possible \nprimarily along and west of I-95. That said, damaging wind gusts \nand hail will remain the greatest threat. Dominant mode should \nbe multicell convection but a supercell can't be ruled out \nespecially where cloud bearing shear is maximized. SPC has \nexpanded the area of a slight risk (2 out of 5) to now include \nmuch of the area. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A cold front will cross the area Friday.\n\nThe timing of this cold front will be critical in the risk for \nthunderstorm hazards. There are differences between models in \nboth the timing, and if this will be a single cold front, or an \ninitial cold front with a reinforcing shot of cold air advection\ncoming in behind it. Saturday and Sunday appear to be dry across\nmost of the area with the exception of southern areas Saturday\nwhere low probabilities of showers and storms will exist.",
